<Project>

  <!--
    This file exists first of all to be found.

    MSBuild imports only the NEAREST Directory.Build.targets above a project. When this
    repository is checked out as a submodule of the Broiler monorepo, the monorepo's root
    Directory.Build.targets would otherwise reach every project here — and it imports
    eng/aggregate-workspace-references.targets, which rewrites a component's relative
    ProjectReferences to point at the monorepo's own top-level component checkouts instead
    of the nested ones beside this file.

    That rewrite is right for a component the monorepo builds from its own solutions. It is
    wrong here, because it aims at the WRONG ROOT: it would send these ProjectReferences to
    the monorepo's checkouts while the solutions in this repository name checkouts under
    this directory, so an aggregate build compiles BOTH the monorepo's Broiler.UI,
    Broiler.Graphics, Broiler.Input and Broiler.Media and the copies here — inventing the
    duplicate assemblies that redirect exists to remove.

    Broiler.Browser shields itself the same way and for the same reason; the generator
    script documents the trap in full, and reports a component that ships this file as
    deliberately not redirected rather than silently emitting redirects that do nothing.

    Do NOT "fix" this by importing the targets file above it. Collapsing this repository
    onto the monorepo's checkouts needs its solutions rewritten as well, which cannot be
    done without breaking the standalone build. It is a real piece of design work, not a
    one-line import.

    That warning deliberately does not spell out the MSBuild function that would perform
    such an import. The monorepo's generator decides whether a component's targets file
    breaks the chain by searching it for that function's NAME, so writing the name here —
    even to forbid it — makes this file look like one that chains upward, and the generator
    then emits the redirects this file exists to prevent. Keep it unnamed.

    What the file DOES do is the same rewrite aimed at the right root. Shielding this
    repository from the monorepo's redirect does not remove its need for one: the component
    checkouts here nest each other (Broiler.UI carries its own Broiler.Input, and so on), so
    the same assembly is reachable by several paths within this directory alone. The
    solutions fold those onto one canonical checkout; without a matching fold on the
    ProjectReferences the build graph still names all of them, NuGet collapses the duplicate
    package identities onto whichever it picks, and an assembly can drop out of a head's
    deps.json — a startup FileNotFoundException for a file sitting next to the exe, from a
    build that reported no errors. eng/fold-duplicate-checkouts.targets closes that, and is
    generated by the same walk that writes the solutions so the two cannot disagree.

    Nothing else belongs here. Component-wide build settings go in Directory.Build.props.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <BroilerRepositoryRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)</BroilerRepositoryRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)eng\fold-duplicate-checkouts.targets" />

</Project>
